What:		/proc/cpu_ctl
Date:		October 2026
Contact:		Custom Android kernel maintainer
Description:
		Available with CONFIG_CPU_PUBLIC_CONTROL=y. The kernel creates
		this node with mode 0666. All users and applications can read
		state and request global CPU hotplug operations, without root,
		capabilities, a UID allowlist, or an exclusive controller.

		Reading returns newline-terminated CPU lists:

		possible: 0-7
		present: 0-7
		online: 0-7
		active: 0-7
		hotpluggable: 0-7

		Send one complete ASCII command in each write:

		  online N       Bring CPU N online.
		  offline N      Take CPU N offline.
		  set CPULIST    Request exactly this online CPU list.

		For example, "set 0-3,6\n" requests CPUs 0,1,2,3,6. The maximum
		write size is 4095 bytes. Embedded NUL bytes and multiple commands
		in one write are not accepted. Repeating an already satisfied
		online/offline request succeeds if the CPU supports the operation.

		Requests use the standard device hotplug lock and device bus
		callbacks, shared with the existing sysfs interface. Replacement
		CPUs are brought online before any other CPUs are taken offline.
		The existing CPU-core protection refuses to offline the last
		active CPU. Empty masks and CPUs that are not present are rejected.
		Protected CPUs and firmware restrictions are not overridden.

		Successful writes return their byte count. Failures return a
		negative errno, including EINVAL (bad command/list), E2BIG (input
		too long), ENODEV (CPU unavailable), EPERM (protected CPU), EBUSY
		(last active CPU or hotplug temporarily unavailable), or a bus/
		firmware error. A batch is not a transaction: failures may leave
		earlier successful operations in effect. Read the node again to
		obtain actual state. Concurrent callers are serialized, without
		an ownership or priority policy.

		SELinux grants read/write, getattr, and the zero-length truncation
		used by shell redirection/FileOutputStream only for the proc inode
		carrying this driver's private marker. The check does not use a
		pathname, UID, or a generic proc label. Execute and permission/
		owner changes are not exempted, nor are other files. SELinux
		enforcing mode remains enabled.

		No hotplug state is forced at boot. Commands change the live CPU
		state; they do not install persistent constraints on firmware,
		thermal management, or vendor CPU policy. Mount namespace hiding,
		read-only mounts, and other security modules remain independent.
